;; EPIC: shift claimgraph from eager pre-assembly to just-in-time construction.
;;
;; Companion to issues.edn (the ordinary backlog) and grep-baseline-benchmark.edn
;; (the harness that decides whether on-demand retrieval should exist at all).
;;
;; Read with: (clojure.edn/read-string (slurp "lazy-rewrite.edn"))

{:epic
 {:title "Lazy / just-in-time graph construction"
  :status :proposed
  :motivation
  "GraphRAG-style pre-assembly of a corpus at write time does not pay for
   itself, and LazyGraphRAG showed most of the quality is recoverable at a
   small fraction of the indexing cost by deferring the expensive semantic
   work to query time. The wiki analogy generalizes it: tokens spent writing
   are capital goods, and the capital does not return unless the content is
   read on the order of 25 times.

   claimgraph is not a wiki and not a RAG index, but it does spend eagerly in
   the same shape — every note is turned into claims by a model call whether
   or not anything ever asks about it, and curation then judges pairs of those
   claims on a schedule rather than on demand.

   Measured on this project's own store 2026-08-08, the capital is not
   returning. See :evidence."}

 :evidence
 {:store-size {:facts 749 :valid 740 :entities 208 :episodes 154
               :open-conflicts 16}
  :retrieval
  {:log ".claimgraph/db.retrievals"
   :events 5
   :distinct-facts-read 7
   :max-reads-of-any-fact 2
   :last-event "2026-07-26 (13 days before measurement)"
   :share-of-facts-ever-read "~0.9%"
   :caveat "See task :t1 — this number is only meaningful if the log records
            every read. It may be under-recording."}
  :grep-baseline
  {:grep-notes-book-docs-ms 22
   :claim-search-ms 166
   :ratio "grep ~7.5x faster"
   :accuracy-note
   "claim search for `reproduce-first review` returns one of six paraphrases
    of a single lesson, flagged as conflicting with two of its own siblings,
    at retrieval-score 0.0103. On that query the graph's answer is worse than
    reading the note, which says the thing once."}
  :injected-view
  {:bytes 12477
   :rows "~17 commitments + 2 conflict clusters + 5 supersessions + 50 facts"
   :read-frequency "every session, unconditionally, without a query"
   :note "The eager pipeline produces ~749 facts to fill ~50 slots."}}

 :design-rule
 "Spend eagerly only on what is either INJECTED or UNOBSERVABLE LATER.
  Everything else defers.

  Injected: the compiled view is read every session without anyone asking, so
  its payback threshold is cleared trivially. It is also already lazy in the
  right way — recomputed deterministically from the graph on each compile,
  with no model call anywhere in it.

  Unobservable later: bi-temporal validity, supersession links, episode
  provenance, and write-time conflict detection record events that stop being
  observable. A RAG index defers safely because the corpus is still there at
  query time; a file that has been overwritten and a session that has ended
  are not. Indexing can be deferred. Observation cannot.

  On-demand retrieval is neither, and it is where the waste concentrates."

 :non-goals
 ["Making the bi-temporal record lazy. Observation is not deferrable."
  "Removing write-time conflict detection. Its whole value is firing while the
   user is present and can act; the same finding six months later is
   archaeology."
  "A format break. Version stays 1, additive data only."
  "Regressing the injected view, which is the strongest leg and the one the
   critique does not touch."]

 :open-questions
 ["Can notes be ranked well enough WITHOUT extraction to decide which ones earn
   a model call? The strongest defence of eager extraction is that filling the
   top 50 requires candidates, and you cannot rank what you never read. If
   cheap ranking (recency, size, mention overlap with high-usage entities)
   fills the view about as well as exhaustive extraction, the epic is
   justified; if it does not, eagerness is load-bearing after all."
  "What does a fact actually cost today, end to end — extraction share plus its
   share of curation? Without that the payback arithmetic stays qualitative."
  "Does anyone use temporal queries? `history` and `recall` exist; the
   retrievals log shows no evidence either way, which is itself task :t1."]

 :tasks
 {:t1
  {:title "Establish whether the retrievals log is trustworthy"
   :size :small
   :blocks [:t2 :t11]
   :description
   "Every number in this epic rests on the claim that 7 of 749 facts have ever
    been read. A log that stopped 13 days ago admits two readings: retrieval is
    genuinely unused, or retrieval logging is broken or bypassed. These call
    for opposite responses, so nothing else should start until it is settled.

    Check which read paths append to the log — CLI verbs, MCP tools/call, the
    skill's own queries, coach — and whether any of them read without
    recording. Confirm the ambient loop's own reads are excluded deliberately
    rather than accidentally."
   :acceptance
   "Either: the log provably records every read path, and the 0.9% figure
    stands. Or: gaps are identified and closed, and the figure is re-measured
    over a fresh window before any design decision cites it."}

  :t2
  {:title "Instrument what the injected view actually earns"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:t1]
   :description
   "The view is read every session by construction, but 'injected' is not the
    same as 'used'. Record which facts occupy the view over time, how often
    its contents change, and how long a fact survives in it. A fact that has
    held a slot for months is capital that returned; one that appeared once
    and fell out paid for a single impression.

    Attribution of downstream use is the hard part and probably not solvable
    directly. Slot-tenure is a good proxy and is cheap."
   :acceptance
   "A report of slot tenure and churn for the view, over enough history to
    distinguish stable capital from noise."}

  :t3
  {:title "Draw the tier boundary explicitly in the ingest path"
   :size :medium
   :description
   "Today capture, extraction, conflict detection and curation are threaded
    through one ingest path, so there is no seam at which deferral can be
    introduced. Separate them by cost class: eager-deterministic (capture),
    eager-cheap (collision pre-filter), deferred-expensive (extraction,
    judging, summarization).

    This is preparatory refactoring and should change no behaviour. Its value
    is that every later task in this epic becomes a local change."
   :acceptance
   "The three classes are separately invocable, the suite is unchanged, and a
    reader can tell from the call graph which class any given model call
    belongs to."}

  :t4
  {:title "Cheap deterministic note index"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:t3]
   :description
   "The substrate deferral needs: for each note, its hash, size, mtime, source
    kind, and the entity mentions found by plain string matching against the
    known roster and its aliases. No model call.

    This is what lets a note be RECORDED without being UNDERSTOOD — enough to
    rank it, route a query to it, and prove it existed, while the expensive
    reading of it waits for a reason."
   :acceptance
   "Every note the pile scan finds is indexed deterministically in time
    comparable to the code pass, and the index is sufficient to answer 'which
    notes might bear on entity X' without extraction."}

  :t5
  {:title "Store notes verbatim as first-class sources"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:t4]
   :description
   "Decouple 'this note exists and here is what it says' from 'here are the
    claims it makes'. The verbatim text plus the cheap index becomes the
    durable record; claims become a derived, regenerable projection.

    This also fixes a present asymmetry: a claim carries a quote receipt, but
    the source it came from is not itself retained, so a re-read means going
    back to a file that may have changed."
   :acceptance
   "A note's content is recoverable from the store at the version that was
    ingested, and claims reference it rather than a path that may have moved."}

  :t6
  {:title "Extract on demand, ranked by cheap signals"
   :size :large
   :depends-on [:t5]
   :blocked-by [:open-question "note ranking without extraction"]
   :description
   "Replace extract-everything-at-write-time with extract-when-needed. Two
    triggers: a compile that is ranking candidates for the injected view, and a
    query whose cheap index routing lands on an unextracted note.

    Ranking must work without extraction — recency, size, source kind, and
    mention overlap with entities that already earn view slots. This is the
    epic's central bet and the open question above.

    Pair with hash-keyed caching (issues.edn :polish and the audit cache idea)
    so a note extracted once is never re-extracted unchanged."
   :acceptance
   "The injected view built from on-demand extraction is materially as good as
    the view built from exhaustive extraction, measured against the same
    store, at a fraction of the model calls. If it is not, record that and
    stop — the bet failed and eagerness was load-bearing."}

  :t7
  {:title "Cheap collision pre-filter so conflict detection stays eager"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:t4]
   :description
   "Write-time contradiction detection is the one feature laziness would
    genuinely destroy, so it stays eager — but it does not need a full
    extraction to fire. Match the incoming note's mentions against subjects
    that already carry standing commitments, compare against existing objects
    by similarity, and escalate to a model call only when a collision looks
    likely.

    This is LazyGraphRAG's cheap-best-first move pointed at conflicts rather
    than community summaries: spend the model only where the cheap signal says
    something is there."
   :acceptance
   "Instruction-conflict and contradiction findings fire at comparable recall
    to today's exhaustive path, on a small fraction of the calls. Recall is the
    metric that matters — a missed contradiction is the failure this feature
    exists to prevent."}

  :t8
  {:title "Make curation read-triggered rather than scheduled"
   :size :large
   :depends-on [:t3]
   :description
   "Curation runs a fixed pass (~34 model calls) judging conflicts and
    enriching entities regardless of whether anything reads the outcome. Judge
    a conflict when someone looks at it, or when it competes for a slot in the
    injected view — the two moments its verdict changes what a human sees.

    Note the interaction with issues.edn :budget-allocation: today the judge is
    starved by extraction. Under this model there is far less extraction to
    starve it."
   :acceptance
   "No model call is made for a conflict nobody has looked at and that is not
    contending for a view slot; the queue the user actually reads is judged at
    least as well as today."}

  :t9
  {:title "Bound or retire the scheduled consolidation pass"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:t8]
   :description
   "Consolidation's ~34 sequential calls already exceeded a 600s hook timeout
    once and made the pass permanently due. Once curation is demand-driven,
    most of what consolidation does on a timer has a trigger instead. What
    remains — closing genuinely stale episodes, summarizing for full-text
    search — should be bounded and incremental rather than whole-store."
   :acceptance
   "No unbounded scheduled model work remains; whatever stays on a timer has a
    per-run bound and makes progress incrementally."}

  :t10
  {:title "Fold the eager code pass into the same rule"
   :size :small
   :description
   "The code pass is deterministic and cheap (1.4s of analysis for 88 files),
    so it is NOT the capital sink and mostly belongs on the eager side. But it
    is currently all-or-nothing on the git ref: one commit re-analyzes the
    whole tree. Make it proportional to the diff.

    Included here because it is the same principle — spend in proportion to
    what changed — even though the deterministic layer is not where the waste
    is. Depends on issues.edn :batched-writes for the write path."
   :acceptance
   "A one-file commit costs analysis and writes proportional to one file."}

  :t11
  {:title "Decide the fate of on-demand retrieval"
   :size :medium
   :depends-on [:t1]
   :see-also "grep-baseline-benchmark.edn"
   :description
   "The benchmark, not this epic, settles whether leg 3 should exist. Three
    outcomes worth planning for:

    KEEP AND SPECIALIZE — the graph wins decisively on temporal, provenance
    and conflict questions and loses point lookups to grep. Then retrieval
    narrows to what grep cannot answer and stops competing on recall of
    literal text.

    KEEP AND FIX — it loses today only because of paraphrase pollution, and
    admission-time dedup (issues.edn :admission-dedup) closes the gap.

    CONCEDE — grep wins broadly enough that retrieval is not worth its share
    of the eager spend, and claimgraph specializes in the two legs grep cannot
    touch: the ambient injected view and the temporal record.

    Conceding is a legitimate outcome and should not be treated as failure —
    it would sharpen the product rather than shrink it."
   :acceptance
   "A decision recorded with the benchmark numbers behind it."}}}
